		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Beginning in eighteenth-century historiography, many came to regard him as the first philosopher in the [[Ancient Greek philosophy|Greek tradition]], breaking from the prior use of [[Mythology|mythology]] to explain the world and instead using [[Natural philosophy|natural philosophy]]. He is thus otherwise referred to as the first to have engaged in [[Mathematics|mathematics]], [[History of science|science]], and [[Deductive reasoning|deductive reasoning]]. Thales's view that all of [[Nature|nature]] is based on the existence of a single ultimate substance, which he [[Theory|theorized]] to be water, was widely influential among the philosophers of his time. Thales thought the Earth floated on water. In mathematics, Thales is the namesake of Thales's theorem, and the intercept theorem can also be referred to as Thales's theorem. Thales was said to have calculated the heights of the pyramids and the distance of ships from the shore. In science, Thales was an astronomer who reportedly [[Weather forecasting|predicted the weather]] and a [[Solar eclipse|solar eclipse]].",
